WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.076
(xylophone music)

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:04.654
Philip: People, what I would like to know

00:00:04.654 --> 00:00:06.236
is the answer to a very simple question.

00:00:06.236 --> 00:00:08.349
Are we alone, as conscious beings,

00:00:08.349 --> 00:00:13.204
in this entire buzzing
400-billion star galaxy,

00:00:13.204 --> 00:00:16.759
one of ten to the tenth other galaxies?

00:00:16.759 --> 00:00:18.862
It seems pretty implausible.

00:00:19.169 --> 00:00:20.185
Brit: The modern search

00:00:20.185 --> 00:00:23.348
for extraterrestrial
intelligence, or SETI,

00:00:23.348 --> 00:00:26.609
began in 1959, when
two Cornell physicists,

00:00:26.609 --> 00:00:29.447
Giuseppe Cocconi and Philip Morrison,

00:00:29.447 --> 00:00:31.100
published an article in Nature

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.698
that outlined the possibility

00:00:32.698 --> 00:00:34.781
of using radio and microwaves

00:00:34.781 --> 00:00:37.949
to communicate between the stars.

00:00:37.949 --> 00:00:39.619
In order for this to work,

00:00:39.619 --> 00:00:43.419
researchers assumed that
any intelligent civilization

00:00:43.419 --> 00:00:45.184
will have discovered the ability

00:00:45.184 --> 00:00:47.347
to transmit radio waves.

00:00:48.439 --> 00:00:50.541
This assumption is based, in part,

00:00:50.541 --> 00:00:52.224
on the fact that it took human beings

00:00:52.224 --> 00:00:55.100
only 80 years to figure
out how to do this,

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.442
following Alessandro Volta's discovery

00:00:57.442 --> 00:00:59.975
of batteries and electric current.

00:00:59.975 --> 00:01:01.680
The premise is quite simple.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.813
We can create radio waves by sending

00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:06.683
short pulses of electric
current through wires.

00:01:06.683 --> 00:01:09.706
These waves can then travel
beyond our atmosphere

00:01:09.706 --> 00:01:13.236
and out through Space
with little interference.

00:01:13.236 --> 00:01:16.140
Once these radio or electromagnetic waves

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.790
are sent out, they can be
received, using antennas,

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.133
and turned back into electrical pulses.

00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:25.420
In 1960, Frank Drake
conducted the first search

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:28.555
for radio signals from
other solar systems.

00:01:28.555 --> 00:01:30.949
Much like turning a radio dial,

00:01:30.949 --> 00:01:33.175
Drake was trying to scan the sky

00:01:33.175 --> 00:01:36.634
in order to tune in to faint radio signals

00:01:36.634 --> 00:01:39.548
that might be coming from other worlds.

00:01:39.548 --> 00:01:41.189
Though this first attempt did not result

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:43.100
in any noteworthy findings,

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.648
researchers have been scanning

00:01:44.648 --> 00:01:47.119
the stars ever since.

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:48.823
Carl: So, there is some chance that,

00:01:48.823 --> 00:01:50.167
in the next few decades,

00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:52.227
we will get the signal

00:01:52.227 --> 00:01:54.949
from some spectacularly distant,

00:01:54.949 --> 00:01:57.694
spectacularly exotic civilization,

00:01:57.694 --> 00:01:59.412
and everything on Earth will,

00:01:59.412 --> 00:02:02.296
as a consequence,
change. That is possible.

00:02:02.296 --> 00:02:05.883
Kent: The interesting
thing about the SETI search

00:02:05.883 --> 00:02:08.667
is that, although we claim
that we're looking for

00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:13.095
extraterrestrial intelligence,
we can't actually define

00:02:13.095 --> 00:02:16.270
what an intelligent signal is, and so,

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:19.327
a starting point is to
say we look for a signal

00:02:19.327 --> 00:02:21.890
which nature will not produce

00:02:21.890 --> 00:02:24.807
by any mechanism that we understand.

00:02:24.807 --> 00:02:27.423
Brit: An important question emerges.

00:02:27.423 --> 00:02:28.662
How can we ever know

00:02:28.662 --> 00:02:30.589
if such a signal is coming

00:02:30.589 --> 00:02:32.728
from an intelligent source?

00:02:32.728 --> 00:02:35.974
At the first SETI meeting, in 1961,

00:02:35.974 --> 00:02:38.836
John Lilly proposed that researchers study

00:02:38.836 --> 00:02:41.453
dolphin languages to help them learn more

00:02:41.453 --> 00:02:43.713
about what extraterrestrial signals

00:02:43.713 --> 00:02:45.758
might be like.

00:02:45.758 --> 00:02:48.482
Much of this early work
culminated in the research

00:02:48.482 --> 00:02:50.449
conducted by Laurance R Doyle

00:02:50.449 --> 00:02:53.677
and Brenda McCowan.
Doyle and McCowan's work

00:02:53.677 --> 00:02:55.479
is based on the assumption that,

00:02:55.479 --> 00:02:58.176
if there is some common
trait, in both human

00:02:58.176 --> 00:03:00.719
and non-human communication systems,

00:03:00.719 --> 00:03:03.230
then extraterrestrial
communication systems

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:06.370
should also share this trait.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:09.610
They analyzed a long
sequence of vocalizations

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:13.526
from both adult and baby
humans, and dolphins,

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:15.195
and, in the case of dolphins,

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:17.965
this was a set of whistles and clicks.

00:03:17.965 --> 00:03:20.903
Now, human babies learn to speak

00:03:20.903 --> 00:03:21.908
(baby babbles)
through a process

00:03:21.908 --> 00:03:25.210
of vocal imitation,
slowly amassing a larger

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:27.835
and larger set of sound signals.

00:03:27.835 --> 00:03:31.211
Baby in green top: (babbling)

00:03:35.441 --> 00:03:38.934
Brit: However, during what is
known as the babbling phase,

00:03:38.934 --> 00:03:41.446
the sounds produced
are more or less random

00:03:41.446 --> 00:03:44.833
or unstructured. To see
this, Doyle and McCowan

00:03:44.833 --> 00:03:46.893
plotted the different sound signals

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:50.189
against their frequency,
or how often they occur,

00:03:50.189 --> 00:03:51.681
then they ordered the symbols

00:03:51.681 --> 00:03:53.756
in the graph according to frequency,

00:03:53.756 --> 00:03:55.862
with the most common symbols on the left

00:03:55.862 --> 00:03:58.096
and the least common on the right.

00:03:58.096 --> 00:04:00.604
With human babies, the
slope is nearly level

00:04:00.604 --> 00:04:02.707
as all sound signals produced

00:04:02.707 --> 00:04:06.117
occur fairly evenly or randomly.

00:04:06.117 --> 00:04:07.989
However, as children learn the language

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:11.453
of their parents, they
narrow their sound repertoire

00:04:11.453 --> 00:04:14.249
to fit the model to
which they are exposed,

00:04:14.249 --> 00:04:15.908
Boy green top: Ah, eh, eh, oo.

00:04:15.908 --> 00:04:19.776
Brit: structure is imposed
on our speech patterns.

00:04:19.776 --> 00:04:22.243
Consequently, the slope of this graph

00:04:22.243 --> 00:04:25.627
converges towards a 45 degree angle,

00:04:25.627 --> 00:04:29.118
or a negative one slope
on a log-log chart.

00:04:29.118 --> 00:04:31.858
This is known as Zipf's Law.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.123
What's interesting is that this same slope

00:04:34.123 --> 00:04:36.712
appears in different human languages

00:04:36.712 --> 00:04:40.978
and seems to be a
pattern all humans share.

00:04:40.978 --> 00:04:43.213
Even more surprising is that this pattern

00:04:43.213 --> 00:04:45.480
also emerged when Doyle and McCowan

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:48.367
analyzed non-human communication.

00:04:48.367 --> 00:04:49.957
They found that the whistle sounds

00:04:49.957 --> 00:04:52.794
produced by baby dolphins
seemed to be distributed

00:04:52.794 --> 00:04:55.357
in a pattern similar to human babies

00:04:55.357 --> 00:04:57.102
during the babbling phase.

00:04:57.102 --> 00:04:58.582
At first, the dolphin whistles

00:04:58.582 --> 00:05:01.295
are more or less unstructured,

00:05:01.295 --> 00:05:03.231
and, by the time they reach adulthood,

00:05:03.231 --> 00:05:05.181
the graph converges on a slope

00:05:05.181 --> 00:05:09.180
of about negative one,
which is the same as humans.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:12.141
(dolphin whistles and clicks)

00:05:12.141 --> 00:05:16.354
This sort of analysis looks
only at individual signals

00:05:16.354 --> 00:05:18.442
or words and doesn't say anything

00:05:18.442 --> 00:05:21.120
about the deeper linguistic structure

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:25.151
of either human or dolphin
communication systems.

00:05:25.151 --> 00:05:26.492
Let's clarify what we mean

00:05:26.492 --> 00:05:28.928
by deeper structure with an example.

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:30.831
If I select a random word from a book

00:05:30.831 --> 00:05:32.998
and ask you to guess what it is,

00:05:32.998 --> 00:05:35.175
you will have no clue what it might be

00:05:35.175 --> 00:05:37.161
and will have to simply guess.

00:05:37.161 --> 00:05:39.961
If, instead, I give you
a random word from a book

00:05:39.961 --> 00:05:43.109
and ask you to predict
the word that follows it,

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:44.826
you will still have to guess.

00:05:44.826 --> 00:05:47.775
However, you'll notice it's likely easier

00:05:47.775 --> 00:05:50.643
to guess this word and,
if I give you a sequence

00:05:50.643 --> 00:05:53.685
of two words from a book,
and ask you to predict

00:05:53.685 --> 00:05:57.572
the third word, it becomes
more predictable still,

00:05:57.572 --> 00:05:59.769
and, if you are given a
sequence of three words,

00:05:59.769 --> 00:06:02.680
this trend continues; the ability to guess

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.443
is even easier. It seems that,

00:06:05.443 --> 00:06:08.004
as a result of the structure of language,

00:06:08.004 --> 00:06:10.659
the freedom of choice decreases

00:06:10.659 --> 00:06:14.745
as we look at longer and
longer strings of words.

00:06:14.745 --> 00:06:17.134
Intuitively, this is why we can finish

00:06:17.134 --> 00:06:19.207
each other's sentences.

00:06:19.207 --> 00:06:22.194
Now, to quantify this, Doyle and McCowan

00:06:22.194 --> 00:06:25.697
borrowed Claude Shannon's
measure of entropy,

00:06:25.697 --> 00:06:29.394
which, as you recall, is
a measure of surprise.

00:06:29.394 --> 00:06:31.555
Entropy can be thought of as the number

00:06:31.555 --> 00:06:34.479
of yes or no questions, or bits,

00:06:34.479 --> 00:06:37.123
required to guess the next word,

00:06:37.123 --> 00:06:39.232
so, as predictability increases,

00:06:39.232 --> 00:06:41.874
the information entropy decreases,

00:06:41.874 --> 00:06:43.531
and Doyle and McCowan

00:06:43.531 --> 00:06:47.711
calculated the entropy for
different depths or orders,

00:06:47.711 --> 00:06:50.542
so, single words is first order,

00:06:50.542 --> 00:06:52.869
groups of two words is second order,

00:06:52.869 --> 00:06:54.884
groups of three words is third order,

00:06:54.884 --> 00:06:57.110
and so on, and then they plotted

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:59.384
the value of information entropy

00:06:59.384 --> 00:07:02.400
against this depth and, for adult humans,

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.105
as we may expect, they found

00:07:04.105 --> 00:07:07.118
that the information entropy decreases

00:07:07.118 --> 00:07:09.528
as the depth increases,

00:07:09.528 --> 00:07:11.469
and this is a result of the rule structure

00:07:11.469 --> 00:07:14.021
in our communication systems.

00:07:14.021 --> 00:07:17.222
Amazingly, Doyle and
McCowan did the same thing

00:07:17.222 --> 00:07:21.080
with dolphin languages and
found the same pattern.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:23.437
Dolphin communication systems display

00:07:23.437 --> 00:07:25.942
decreasing information entropy

00:07:25.942 --> 00:07:29.978
as we look at longer
sequences of sound signals.

00:07:29.978 --> 00:07:32.870
This means that, in dolphin
communication systems,

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:35.412
there is a rule structure which emerges

00:07:35.412 --> 00:07:38.209
and, arguably, this also allows dolphins

00:07:38.209 --> 00:07:40.433
to finish each other's sentences too.

00:07:40.433 --> 00:07:43.366
(dolphin whistles and clicks)

00:07:45.052 --> 00:07:47.692
Now contrast this to
just a random sequence

00:07:47.692 --> 00:07:49.984
of symbols which has a flat line

00:07:49.984 --> 00:07:52.121
on this information entropy graph,

00:07:52.121 --> 00:07:54.258
since there is no conditional dependence

00:07:54.258 --> 00:07:57.802
between symbols. Now, because this pattern

00:07:57.802 --> 00:08:00.204
emerges in both human and non-human

00:08:00.204 --> 00:08:02.871
communication systems, Doyle and McCowan

00:08:02.871 --> 00:08:06.614
have suggested that this
decreasing of entropy

00:08:06.614 --> 00:08:08.736
is essential for the transmission

00:08:08.736 --> 00:08:11.200
of what we might call knowledge.

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:14.959
As Doyle puts it, "If we
get a narrow band signal,

00:08:14.959 --> 00:08:17.687
"a negative one slope on the Zipf plot,

00:08:17.687 --> 00:08:20.438
"and higher order Shannon entropies,

00:08:20.438 --> 00:08:22.609
"we've nailed it." 

00:08:22.609 --> 00:08:25.102
and all of this rests on a simple premise

00:08:25.102 --> 00:08:29.124
that aliens, too, can finish
each other's sentences.

00:08:29.124 --> 00:08:31.317
Man in film: How can I help
you to communicate with us?

00:08:31.317 --> 00:08:33.528
Alien in film: Now I am able to speak

00:08:33.528 --> 00:08:37.731
by assimilation, a form of photosynthesis.

00:08:37.731 --> 00:08:39.154
I have been able to incorporate

00:08:39.154 --> 00:08:43.557
certain of Dr [Wyman's]
functional processes.

00:08:43.557 --> 00:08:45.855
Man in film: Was Dr
Wyman's death necessary?

00:08:45.855 --> 00:08:47.924
Alien in film: Through his sacrifice,

00:08:47.924 --> 00:08:49.614
I can communicate.

00:08:50.136 --> 00:08:53.089
Brit: Without even
understanding the language

00:08:53.089 --> 00:08:57.120
or culture of the other
human or non-human species,

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.865
Claude Shannon's entropy
is a unit of measure

00:08:59.865 --> 00:09:02.501
that can allow us to detect the presence

00:09:02.501 --> 00:09:06.239
of these structural rules,
regardless of meaning.

00:09:06.239 --> 00:09:09.613
Claude Shannon's model of information

00:09:09.613 --> 00:09:12.737
was borne out of a desire to save time

00:09:12.737 --> 00:09:15.489
over the telegraph wires,

00:09:15.489 --> 00:09:18.853
and this led to the global
unit of information,

00:09:18.853 --> 00:09:20.941
the bit, a single difference,

00:09:20.941 --> 00:09:24.483
now the backbone of our
information economy.

00:09:24.483 --> 00:09:27.530
The increasingly digital
and network technologies

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:30.681
that drive our modern
world point to the power

00:09:30.681 --> 00:09:33.892
and persistence of Claude Shannon's ideas.

00:09:33.892 --> 00:09:35.852
The bit is here to stay,

00:09:35.852 --> 00:09:37.971
and the study of information theory

00:09:37.971 --> 00:09:39.877
will continue to play a key role

00:09:39.877 --> 00:09:43.359
in our technological
and social innovations,

00:09:43.359 --> 00:09:45.808
on Earth and, perhaps, beyond.

00:09:45.808 --> 00:09:47.308
Carl: I think even if
there's a plausible argument

00:09:47.308 --> 00:09:49.918
for a few, we've got to keep looking.

00:09:49.918 --> 00:09:51.664
I'd even go further than that.

00:09:51.664 --> 00:09:52.784
If there's a plausible argument

00:09:52.784 --> 00:09:54.672
that there isn't anybody out there,

00:09:54.672 --> 00:09:56.625
bearing in mind that we can be wrong,

00:09:56.625 --> 00:09:59.023
we ought to keep looking,
because the question

00:09:59.023 --> 00:10:02.076
is of the most supreme importance.

00:10:02.076 --> 00:10:04.610
It calibrates our place in the Universe.

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:08.329
It tells us who we are, and so,

00:10:08.329 --> 00:10:11.660
it is worthwhile trying to
find other civilizations,

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:14.747
so, I would say no matter what.

